.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1147 AM PDT Thu Sep 3 2026
(Thursday through Thursday night)

Cloud cover will increase through the day as a cold front approaches
from the northwest. FROPA will occur this afternoon into this
evening from northwest to southeast. The standard wind shift from
southeasterly to northwesterly will occur pre and post-frontal,
respectively, with gusts up to 25 mph to be expected. Gusts of 30-40
mph are expected through terrain favored gaps and passes such as The
Delta, Altamont Pass, and Pacheco Pass, as well as down the Salinas
Valley. For the North Bay, rainfall totals up to 0.25" with locally
higher amounts up to 0.75" in the higher terrain are expected. South
of the Golden Gate Bridge, less than 0.10" is expected with
accumulation favoring the coast and higher terrain with the help of
orographic lift. Overperformance is still a possibility with the
struggles of guidance/models in the early season. While rainfall is
expected to be beneficial, wet roadways will create slick driving
conditions and increase stopping distance. With ample low-level
moisture and post-frontal clearing, the marine layer will try to
reform tonight. As a result, patchy fog/stratus will be possible for
the Friday morning commute.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1147 AM PDT Thu Sep 3 2026
(Friday through Wednesday)

The most impactful weather phenomenon Friday through Monday will be
hazardous conditions at Pacific Coast beaches. Two sets of long
period southerly swell from distant Hurricane Karina and Hurricane
Marie will result in an increased risk for sneaker waves and strong
rip currents with breaking waves up to 10 feet. With pleasant
sensible weather expected, these are the type of days that are most
deadly; with it being the Labor Day holiday weekend, that will
likely only make matters worse. If you want an outdoor activity this
weekend, hit the trails instead of the beach. There`s good agreement
through the weekend that there will be an upper-level low over the
Pacific Northwest which will keep our region under the influence of
troughing. As such, expect below normal temperatures and onshore
flow to continue with the return of the marine layer. The forecast
is now dry this weekend with the farther west track of Marie and dry
air filtering in from the northwest. Uncertainty arrives Monday in
terms of low versus trough, but global ensemble clusters are in
agreement that the pattern becomes progressive again as the low
begins to fill and move east. This will allow for ridging to build
in from the Desert Southwest, kicking off the gradual warming and
drying trend. It is now looking likely that at least some of Marie`s
remnant moisture will eventually be brought into our area as soon as
Tuesday. Whether this transpires as clouds or rainfall will be
dependent on the reinforcing low/trough and the strength of the
flanking ridges. Right now, the official forecast has light rainfall
Tuesday through Thursday favoring the Central Coast.

