.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1101 AM PDT Thu Sep 3 2026

Inland terminals are mostly VFR with sub-MVFR conditions across the
North Bay and near the coast as a weak frontal boundary approaches
the region. The greatest potential for -RA will be at KSTS this
morning and through early afternoon before the boundary sags
southward throughout the afternoon and early evening. -RA is also
possible at terminals to the south, but little to no mensurable
rainfall is expected south of the Golden Gate. Onshore winds will
increase a bit this afternoon in wake of the frontal passage with
VFR conditions returning. Tonight, the marine layer is forecast to
deepen to around 2000 feet and will see MVFR CIGs along the coast
with the potential for sub-MVFR CIGs early Friday morning. VFR
conditions are anticipated after or around 18Z Friday.

Vicinity of SFO...VFR. Onshore winds are forecast to increase this
afternoon and early evening in wake of the frontal passage with
gusts up to 20 kt. There is the potential for MVFR conditions as the
front passes through between 20Z Thursday-03Z Friday. Winds ease
slightly after sunset. There is greater confidence for MVFR CIGs (40-
60% probability) to develop early Friday morning and persist through
16Z Friday before lifting to VFR. Northwesterly winds once again
increase Friday afternoon.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...VFR. Onshore winds are forecast to increase
this afternoon and early evening. The frontal boundary is forecast
to gradually dissipate as it approaches the region late tonight and
into early Friday morning. As such, expecting MVFR conditions to
develop by late evening and lower to IFR (40-60% probability) into
early Friday morning. There is less than 10-30% chance of reaching
LIFR conditions between 08-15Z Friday, however have left out of the
current TAF package. VFR conditions are anticipated after or around
18Z Friday.

